# What the borg manages, so that a drop is noticed.
#
# The nightly cannot use pass and fail for this. Every run dies -- that is what
# unaided play at these levels looks like -- and a run that hits the clock has
# not failed either, so the job goes red only for a crash or a wedge. Without
# this file the fleet could halve overnight and the job would stay green, which
# is exactly the regression the nightly exists to catch.
#
# scripts/borg-progress -b tests/borg/BASELINE compares a sweep against these
# and fails if the fleet has dropped materially. Same shape as
# tests/saves/EXPECTED-FAILURES: a committed statement of what is true now,
# which somebody has to change on purpose.
#
# === Totals decide; rows report ===
#
# The four totals below are the gate. The twelve run: rows are *not*: they exist
# so a failure can say "Warrior seed 1: depth 8->3" instead of leaving a reader
# to diff two files. A row moving is never by itself a regression.
#
# That split is measured rather than assumed. The nightly used to gate on best
# depth and best level, which are *maxima* over the twelve runs -- so they
# report whichever run got luckiest. Eight of the twelve never leave depth 1 on
# any night, so the entire depth signal lives in four runs and best depth lived
# in one. On 9 September that one run's random stream shifted, the headline fell
# from 8 to 3, and the job went red for a change that left the fleet's character
# levels at 39 against 41 and its casting at 14 against 15. That was the only
# false alarm in four nights of real runs, and it came from that rule.
#
# Checked in both directions before replacing it, against those four nights plus
# deliberate regressions: casting collapsing the way the BORG_SPELL_UNKNOWN bug
# made it (4 cast against 15), nothing being learned, the fleet ceasing to
# level, the fleet ceasing to descend, and every character level halved. The
# totals raise no false alarm and miss none of those. The old rules missed none
# either -- they simply cried wolf twice.
#
# === Thresholds ===
#
#   spells cast       less than half         -- caught the one real regression
#   spells learned    less than half            in this file's whole history
#   sum-level         down more than a third
#   sum-depth         down more than two fifths
#
# Depth is the loosest on purpose: with two thirds of the runs stuck on the
# first floor it has almost no room to move and is close to a dead metric today.
# Tighten it when the borg reliably gets below depth 1; until then the character
# levels carry the weight.
#
# === Updating it ===
#
# Take it from a nightly, not from your own machine. Run the workflow by hand
# (Actions -> Borg -> Run workflow) and copy the figures out of its log: the four
# totals from the summary, and the twelve rows from the run table, in the form
# run:CLASS:SEED:DEPTH:CLEVEL.
#
# This is not fussiness. The first version of this file was taken from a local
# sweep, and it cost a night: every number differed from CI's and the job called
# a regression that was not there. The runs are exact to the turn on one machine
# and are not portable between two -- Warrior seed 13 at the same commit played
# 280,358 turns to character level 13 on Darwin arm64 and 5,252 turns to level 1
# on Linux x86_64. A baseline has to be generated the same way it will be
# measured. borg-progress records the platform below and refuses to compare
# across platforms, so a repeat of that says so instead of failing the build.
#
# Updating the rows alone is free -- they decide nothing. Changing a total is
# the part that wants a reason in the commit message.
#
# Do not update it to make a red night green. If a total dropped and you do not
# know why, that is the finding.
#
# === What these numbers depend on ===
#
# The class list, the seeds, the turn budget and the machine -- all four are part
# of what is being measured, so changing any of them invalidates the file.
#
# Data changes reshuffle the random stream and move which run happens to do well;
# a change scoped to something the runs do not touch moves nothing at all. These
# runs set no race, so every character is a Human, and the twelve races added on
# 7 September left all twelve rows byte-identical.

# Taken from the nightly of 9 September 2026, at c7a6e5498, unaided (no cheats,
# no grants, no pets). Twelve runs: Warrior, Mage, Priest and Warrior-Mage
# against seeds 1, 7 and 13, all twelve dying, none capped.
#
# Reshaped from best-depth/best-level to totals-plus-rows in the same commit,
# and the totals are what that night actually measured. The previous file's
# best-depth of 8 came from Warrior seed 1, whose stream moved with the
# pass-wall commit; its run now reads depth 3, level 9.

